#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175760 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "11d9077c-a0ac-5d42-9616-b8fe867d1db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9076" :question/problem "Consider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle$.\nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\,$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/f328d63e617baaed38300a4f68a3df63.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}} = \\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left(e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/e8dfd43252a2b65794b5a03ca7b3995d.png)\n\nSince\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 2, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(2y + \\ln (\\sqrt{x + 5}))dx + (e^{y/3} + 3x)dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3 - 2)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)dA &= ∬_(Δ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/2}^{2π}∫_{0}^{3}rdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175801 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C} \\left(e^{-x^2}-y^3\\right) \\mathrm{d}x + (e^{-y^2}+x^3) \\: \\mathrm{d}y$, where the path $C$ is shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/2e2cf25681d84163eb1887aebdea07b0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/80b659f9413d74a0171fcf45372ad34d.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}-y^3, \\qquad Q(x,y) = e^{-y^2}+x^3\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} =-3y^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3x^{2}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(e^{-x^{2}} - y^{3})dx + (e^{-y^{2}} + x^{3})dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3x^{2} - (-3y^{2}))dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)3(x^{2} + y^{2})dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(3x^{2} + y^{2})dA &= ∬_(Δ)3(r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{2}3r^{3}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ)drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{2}3r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}[\\frac{3r^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}12dθ \\\\\n&= 12[θ]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 12π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175802 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551" :question/problem "Given that\n$AAAAAAAA$\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}} = \\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/629b2ed342ba6afd917f54d19b0f1b7a.png)\n\nSince\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 2y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3y\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(y^{2} + \\sin x)dx + (xy - \\cos y)dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3y - 2y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)ydA &= ∬_(Δ)r\\sin θrdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}∫_{0}^{2}r 2 sin ⁡ θdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c49935bc-a081-5d97-8f9f-7ef80e86caff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\cos θ∫_{0}^{2}2rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173533} {:answer/id #uuid "eeeb905b-3707-59e7-86d7-d99641d6727f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{2}4rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173534} {:answer/id #uuid "48b97ca4-b908-541c-8439-2bdcf2a34f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{2}(r^{3} - e^{\\cos θ})rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173535} {:answer/id #uuid "6c137b1e-80cb-5986-ade2-a4a125dab8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}∫_{0}^{2}4rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173536} {:answer/id #uuid "7d322f5f-a2d0-5f52-9d67-a24594304976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}∫_{0}^{2}(r^{3} - e^{\\cos θ})drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c137b1e-80cb-5986-ade2-a4a125dab8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}∫_{0}^{2}4rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173536} :answer-field/id #uuid "def3c1fd-49fc-548b-beba-697af7210a23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173532}] :question/id #uuid "e6e3c388-6148-5c9d-8b7f-1f6ea60657b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-108544" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/q-108544.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n$∮_(C)(3y - e^{\\cos x})dx + (7x + \\sqrt{y + 5})dy$?"}]}
